# =========================================================================
# orch.bcyr — benchmarks for the orchestration group.
#
# **Six oracle bench files land here** — rust-old/benches/scheduler.rs,
# pubsub.rs, approval.rs, ipc.rs, orchestrator.rs and relay.rs. The rest of the
# file has no oracle counterpart and measures what a crew run actually hammers:
# the event fan-out on every task boundary, the per-task ranking and delegation,
# the plan-cache key, durable state, the prompt guard and the auth check.
#
# ⚠ **relay.rs is a FLEET oracle bench** (rust-old/src/fleet/relay.rs), not an
# orchestrator one, and `src/` mirrors `rust-old/` file-for-file. Its six rows
# sit here because this file was the assigned target and `src/fleet/relay.cyr`
# is a standalone include. `benches/fleet.bcyr` landed in the same milestone and
# covers `fleet.rs` and `placement.rs`; **the six `relay_*` rows belong there and
# should move as one block**, which is a decision to take deliberately rather
# than discover later. No id collides either way — checked across all `.bcyr`.
# And wherever they land, `.bcyr` files stay FLAT in `benches/`: `cyrius bench`
# no-arg discovery is not recursive.
#
# ⚠ **The relay rows are majra numbers reached through agnosai's wrapper.**
# Every function in `src/fleet/relay.cyr` is a one-to-three-line delegation (its
# own header says so), so a regression in `relay_send_*`, `relay_receive_*` or
# `relay_broadcast_*` belongs to lib/majra.cyr unless it is one of the exactly
# three things the wrapper owns: the 24-byte stats re-pack on top of majra's
# 48-byte one, the `str_from("")` allocated on every broadcast where majra
# passes a bare 0, and the capacity field that is recorded and then ignored.
# `relay_broadcast_1_sub_drained` beside `relay_send_1_sub_drained` prices the
# second of those; `relay_stats_snapshot` prices the first.
#
# ⚠ **A subscribed relay channel MUST be drained inside the loop.** majra's
# `relay_send` fans out with a BLOCKING `chan_send` into a hardcoded 256-deep
# channel, so the oracle's subscribe-and-never-read shape — safe on a tokio
# broadcast, which overwrites — would park this process on FUTEX_WAIT at
# iteration 257 and hang with no output at all. Same reasoning as
# `event_round_trip_1_sub` below. `relay_send_no_subs` isolates the fan-out cost
# instead of pretending the drain is free.
#
# ⚠ **Iteration counts here are MEMORY-bound, not time-bound.** `alloc()` is a
# no-free global bump and nothing in the added groups has an `_a` variant to
# thread, so every allocation inside a timed loop is retained for the life of
# the process. That — not the clock — is what sets `ipc_frame_1mib_transport` at
# 50 and `orchestrator_new` at 20000.
#
# ⚠ **MEASURED peak RSS for this file: ~3.13 GB** (2026-08-11, `zsh TIMEFMT %M`).
# An audit found 3.44 GB and pointed out that the sentence above justified only
# two of 72 rows while the rest were sized on per-call estimates that were 2-3x
# low. Two mis-sized rows were then cut — `scheduler_load_dag_linear_50`
# 5000 -> 1000 (~58 KB/call, ~285 MB, and it was LARGER than the 500-task sibling
# that had already been cut for this reason) and the four `pattern_match_*` rows
# 4x (~69 MB each) — which recovered ~230 MB.
#
# **The remaining ~3 GB is spread across all 72 rows, not concentrated.** It is
# recorded rather than fixed: CI runs bare `cyrius bench` on `ubuntu-latest`
# (16 GB), so it holds, but anyone adding a row here should price its allocation
# and not only its timing resolution. Measure with
# `zsh -c 'TIMEFMT="%M"; time (cyrius bench benches/orch.bcyr)'` — `pgrep -P`
# will not see it, because the compiled bench binary is a grandchild. The second ceiling is the audit ring:
# `agnosai_audit_record` evicts with `vec_remove(entries, 0)` AND re-hashes the
# evicted entry once the chain passes AGNOSAI_AUDIT_MAX_ENTRIES = 10000, so the
# `run_crew` rows are capped where they still measure `run_crew` rather than the
# ring shift (3 audit entries per iteration at 1 task, 12 at 10 sequential).
#
# **Fan-out is measured as a round trip.** A subscriber's ring is 256 deep, so a
# send-only loop saturates it after 256 iterations and then measures nothing but
# the eviction path. Draining inside the loop keeps the reader in step and gives
# the number a real deployment sees. `event_send_evicting` measures the other
# case deliberately, against a ring left full on purpose: that is the cost of
# the overwrite-oldest guarantee blocker #4 was about, and the number worth
# knowing is that a stalled SSE client does not get more expensive to serve.
#
# **Logging is turned down to SK_FATAL.** `agnosai_delegate_tasks` emits an INFO
# line per call, matching the oracle's `info!`, and `orch_pubsub` and the event
# bus log on their rejection paths. Left at the default level a delegation
# benchmark measures sakshi writing to a pipe, not the delegation. The log cost
# is real in production and is worth its own number one day; it is not what
# these shapes are for.
#
# These are CYRIUS baseline numbers, NOT comparable to
# rust-old/bench-history.csv. See CLAUDE.md.
#
# Stdlib is auto-prepended from cyrius.cyml — do NOT include stdlib modules.
# =========================================================================

